{:edb/db-before-t 165 :edb/db-after-t 166 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533478 50 #inst "2026-10-11T02:44:13.219Z" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059693 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399506 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059693 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399513 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059694 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399520 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399479 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059703 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399472 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186059775 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399617 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057000 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186060112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399391 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060112 1071 "$3 \\mid 18$ or $11$ is not prime.\n\nWhat is the negation of the compound statement above?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186060112 1071 "$\\,$ $3\\,|\\,18$ or $11$ is not prime.\n\nWhat is the negation of the compound statement above?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060112 1074 "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that the negation of a disjunction of two statements is equivalent to the conjunction of the negations of the individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n¬(A∨B)=¬A∧¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(3\\,|\\,18\\text{ or }11\\text{ is not prime}) & ≡¬((3\\,|\\,18)∨(11\\text{ is not prime})) \\\\  & ≡¬(3\\,|\\,18)∧¬(11\\text{ is not prime}) \\\\  & ≡(3\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,18)∧(11\\text{ is prime}) \\\\  & ≡3\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,18\\text{ and }11\\text{ is prime}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057218 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186060147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060147 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060147 1071 "Given that the minor of $p_{23}$ is $t^{2} + 12$, find all the possible values of $t$ in the matrix\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}t & -8 & 3 \\\\ -7 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186060147 1071 "Given that the minor of ${p}_{23}$ is ${t}^{2}+12$, find all the possible values of $t$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}t & -8 & 3 \\\\ -7 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060147 1074 "First, we remove the second row and third column:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}t & -8 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & 4 \\\\ -1 & 9 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${p}_{23}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}t & -8 \\\\ -1 & 9\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}t & -8 \\\\ -1 & 9\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =t\\cdot 9-(-8)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =9t-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${p}_{23}$ is ${t}^{2}+12$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9t-8 & ={t}^{2}+12 \\\\ {t}^{2}-9t+20 & =0 \\\\ (t-5)(t-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $t=4$ and $t=5.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186057289 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186060156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399527 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060156 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060156 1071 "$\\,$ Either you do not water the plants, or they will grow.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186060156 1071 "Either you do not water the plants, or they will grow.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186060156 1074 "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You do not water the plants.\n\n- $B:$ The plants will grow.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You water the plants.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you water the plants, then they will grow.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ If you water the plants, they will grow.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399596 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186064376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399603 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186068906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399377 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186083246 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399624 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087731 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399356 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186087844 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399384 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096620 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399411 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186096790 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399582 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098819 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186101253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399432 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101253 1071 "Find the general solution to the following equation.\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{y + 2x^{2}y^{2}}{x}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution $z = y^{-1}$.*" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186101253 1071 "Find the general solution to the following equation. \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y+2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{x}\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution $z={y}^{-1}.$*" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101253 1074 "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-1}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{-1}\\,\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{z}^{-1}+2{x}^{2}{z}^{-2}}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{xz}+\\frac{2x}{{z}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{z}{x}-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{z}{x}-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z & =-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-2x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-2x) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =-2{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int 2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-2{x}^{3}}{3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =\\frac{C-2{x}^{3}}{3x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{3x}{C-2{x}^{3}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098849 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186101258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399438 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101258 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101258 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + (y(y - 1))/(x) = 0, y(1) = 5, x > 0$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation\n$\\frac{dz}{dx} + \\frac{1}{x}z = \\frac{1}{x}$\nusing the substitution $z = y^{-1}$.*" 13194139533478 17592186045585 false] [17592186101258 1071 "Solve the initial value problem \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y(y-1)}{x}=0,\\,y(1)=5,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nusing the substitution $z={y}^{-1}.$*" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186101258 1074 "The substitution $z={y}^{-1}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>0.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =x+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{x+C}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =\\frac{x+C}{x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now determine $C$ from the initial condition  $y(1)=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =\\frac{1}{1+C}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{(x-\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x}{(5x-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186115197 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399589 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186124663 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399363 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186129175 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399370 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152057 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399444 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152057 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399451 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152086 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399404 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152088 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399397 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152088 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399561 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399540 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399547 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399554 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152093 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399568 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186152093 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399575 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186184354 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399610 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399458 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399465 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198267 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399533 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198463 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399418 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186198463 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399425 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399357 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1069 "q-16645" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1071 "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1072 #uuid "ba3205c4-c133-5ac7-861e-655b59a14526" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399356 1074 "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{1}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2}+25 & =9\\cdot 25 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2}+25 & =225 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2} & =200 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =\\frac{200}{9} \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{200}{9}} \\\\ x & =-1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-1-\\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)$ and $(-1+\\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399358 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399359 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399360 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399361 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399362 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399360 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399357 1080 #uuid "d58b31e7-b6c4-5d90-9a58-b8914d59f384" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399358 1081 "There is no $x$-intercept" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399358 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399358 1084 #uuid "0e455adf-7973-5ee4-bb3c-0bd83316580a" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399359 1081 "(1\\pm \\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399359 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399359 1084 #uuid "d3568acd-90d7-5e24-95a2-7dddb6de4815" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399360 1081 "(-1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399360 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399360 1084 #uuid "d33bd951-6cf6-508d-8421-15a11016a317" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399361 1081 "(\\pm \\sqrt{5},0)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399361 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399361 1084 #uuid "829db562-f4d5-5e31-b45c-b397742cb7e4" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399362 1081 "(1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399362 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399362 1084 #uuid "a69ed72a-53dd-5923-8c05-9efa0f54638b" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399363 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399364 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399363 1069 "q-167165" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399363 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399363 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=1-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16}.$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399363 1072 #uuid "e7d00266-1ded-5f4c-819a-7467528496c3" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399363 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-2x,\\,2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2x=0 \\\\ 2y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =1-16{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 32\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points:\n\n$$\n(4,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(-4,0),\\,(0,-4)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(4,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(-4,0)$ | $(0,-4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $-15$ | $17$ | $-15$ | $17$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-15$ at the points $(4,0)$ and $(-4,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $17$ at the points $(0,4)$ and $(0,-4).$" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399364 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399365 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399364 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399366 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399364 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399367 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399364 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399368 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399364 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399369 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399364 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399364 1077 "selection" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399364 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399366 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399364 1080 #uuid "bc59060d-a9ed-5533-b31b-311227554f8c" 13194139533478 17592186045585 true] [17592186399365 1081 "${f}_{min}=-3$ and  ${f}_{max}=15$" 13194139533478